🏌️ PGA VenueDNA Tier Engine — 3M Open 2026
TPC Twin Cities | Blaine, MN | July 23–26, 2026 | Defending Champ: Kurt Kitayama (-23, 261)

1. FULL COURSE PROFILE
Vitals: Par 71 (35-36) | 7,431 yards | Architect: Arnold Palmer (opened 2000) | Renovation consultant: Tom Lehman (2018 "competitive enhancement") | Fairways: Creeping Bentgrass | Rough: Kentucky Bluegrass | Greens: Creeping Bentgrass | Elevation change: minimal (267m–282m, only 15m/~49ft total relief) — this is a flat, prairie-style parkland layout, not a hill course
.

Renovation history/key structural notes: TPC Twin Cities hosted the PGA Tour Champions' 3M Championship from 1991–2018 (senior tour venue, considered "easy"). When the PGA Tour Open event was awarded in 2018, Arnold Palmer Design Co., PGA Tour Design Services (Steve Wenzloff), and Minnesota native Tom Lehman ran a 45-day "competitive-enhancement project" starting the day after the final Champions event in Aug 2018. Changes: lengthened the course from ~7,164 yards to 7,431+ yards, narrowed rough/fairway corridors, releveled mounding, added/repositioned bunkers, and converted hole #3 from a 546-yard par-5 to a ~500-yard par-4 (adding difficulty). The most significant structural changes were on the closing stretch: 16th – rough replaced with bentgrass collection areas, a bunker moved 30 yards deeper into the approach zone, fairway bunkers shifted to narrow the tee shot; 17th – new tee boxes added; 18th – the fairway lake was doubled in size, dramatically shrinking the layup zone and increasing risk on the closing par-5. Net effect: what was once one of the senior tour's easier tracks became a genuine go-low birdiefest for bombers/ball-strikers but with teeth on the closing holes.

