Winning scores average roughly -19.4, confirming a consistent birdiefest environment; GIR rates run ~71%, and approach shots have leaned longer than typical "birdiefest" venues (46% of approaches from 175+ yards over recent editions). This is a course that yields plenty of low scores but still separates fields via ball-striking precision rather than pure scrambling luck.

3. DRIVING CORRIDORS & PAR-5 REACHABILITY
Fairway width: ~16th-widest average fairways on Tour (~35 yds avg) — generous relative to Tour average, but water hazards threaten 9 of 14 driving holes, making "avoid the wet stuff" more important than raw width.

Driving distance at TPC Twin Cities skews only slightly above Tour average; the course rewards Total Driving (distance + accuracy combined) more than bombing alone. DataGolf's own course-insight tags rate the venue "Neutral" for both Accuracy and Bombers across 2019–2025 — i.e., no extreme skew either way.

Par-5s (6, 12, 18): All in the 593–594-yard range — long by Tour par-5 standards. Reachability is moderate, favoring legitimate bombers rather than the entire field; 18 in particular is a calculated risk/reward hole given the enlarged lake guarding the green.

4. PRESSURE HOLES
13 (par-3, 228 yds): Longest par-3 on the card — demanding long-iron/hybrid pressure hole mid-back-nine.

16 (par-4, 411 yds), renovated: Narrowed tee shot, tightened green surrounds — a classic "hold your nerve" hole in contention.

17 (par-3, 202 yds), renovated tees: Penultimate-hole pressure test.

18 (par-5, 596 yds): The marquee decision hole — enlarged lake squeezes the layup and creates real eagle/disaster variance in the game's final moments (decisive in the 2025 finish).

5. FAIRWAYS, ROUGH, GREENS & SHORT GAME
Fairways: Creeping Bentgrass — firm, consistent, good roll-out; typical of TPC-network turf.

Rough: Kentucky Bluegrass — moderate penalty, not U.S. Open-style thick; playable recovery lies for accurate misses.

Greens: Creeping Bentgrass — receptive, and per DataGolf, TPC Twin Cities rates among the least difficult putting surfaces on Tour, which somewhat mitigates elite-putting advantage (mediocre putters can still hole out). Historically, greens have run soft in years with rainfall influx, further boosting scoring (2025 preview noted soft greens contributing to low scores).

Short game: With generous, receptive bentgrass greens and moderate rough, scrambling is aided by conditions, but the course's real differentiator is approach play, not short-game wizardry.

Firmness: Generally moderate-to-soft given Minnesota's summer humidity and irrigation; not a hard, running, British-Open-style venue.

6. WHAT TYPE OF GOLF WINS HERE
Winner/elite-finisher profile — Strokes Gained: Approach the Green is king.

All 6 winners (2019–2024) ranked top-20 in SG: Approach and SG: Ball-Striking; 6 of 7 winners (2019–2025) ranked inside the top 10 in SG: Approach — one of only 12 PGA Tour events with 6+ winners ranking top-10 in that category.

Winners have combined for +53.42 strokes gained approaching the green since 2019 — the most of any Tour event in that span. Kitayama's 2025 win featured a personal-best +9.67 SG:Approach for the week.

Accurate/Total Driving matters more than raw bomb-and-gouge given 9 of 14 tee shots flirt with water — course rewards controlled power over max distance.

Putting is a "necessary but not decisive" wildcard — some winners (Cam Champ 2021, Michael Thompson 2020) had career-level hot putting weeks; others (Matthew Wolff, Tony Finau) won primarily by ball-striking with merely average putting. Because greens putt "easy," a poor putter isn't eliminated, but a hot week on the greens can be a difference-maker on the margins.

Middle-iron precision (150–225 yards) is a recurring key stat given the approach-distance distribution skews slightly longer than typical birdiefest venues.

Winner Traits List:

Elite/top-20 Strokes Gained: Approach (near-universal trait)

Strong Total Driving (accuracy + above-average distance combo)

Comfort with mid-to-long iron approaches (150–225 yds)

Ability to go low in bunches (course yields streaky, low rounds — 60s posted twice in 2025 alone)

Composure on closing risk/reward holes (16-17-18)

Anti-patterns / who struggles:

Pure bombers with erratic accuracy off the tee (water threatens 9 driving holes — poor accuracy gets punished more than at a wide-open bomber's paradise)

Short-game-only specialists lacking ball-striking (course profile rewards tee-to-green precision over scrambling — "short gamers" underperform "ball strikers" in the aggregate Tour-wide data, and this bias is amplified here)

Players who rely on putting to bail out mediocre ball-striking weeks — greens are easy enough that elite putting alone rarely separates a winner

Poor iron players from 175+ yards, given the longer-than-average approach